WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.520
Evidence collection needs organization.

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:07.460
Teams often work from evidence request lists, artifact owners, repositories,

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:12.320
naming conventions, version tracking, review status, approval workflows,

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.220
and collection schedules.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:19.160
The mechanics matter because assessment and monitoring depend on being able to locate the

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.660
right artifact,

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.880
understand what it represents,

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:27.420
and determine whether it is current and complete enough for the question being asked.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.440
Artifact ownership is a practical control.

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.340
Someone should be responsible for producing, maintaining, explaining,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.960
and refreshing each important evidence type.

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.500
Engineering may own configuration outputs.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.760
Security may own scan results or incident records.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.420
Operations may own backup checks or monitoring evidence.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.040
Compliance may coordinate review status.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.100
Without owners, evidence often becomes stale, scattered,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.560
or difficult to explain under review pressure.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.580
Sensitive evidence handling deserves deliberate planning.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.440
Evidence may contain vulnerability details, network architecture, identities,

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:08.300
security tooling, customer information, incident records, or operational weaknesses.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:13.240
Repositories should use access control, appropriate retention, version history,

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:15.060
and review workflows.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:18.640
Teams should understand which artifacts can be broadly shared,

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.700
which require tighter handling,

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:25.000
and how to provide evidence without exposing unnecessary sensitive detail.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:28.180
Evidence should be prepared for multiple audiences.

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:30.400
Assessors need to evaluate controls.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:32.620
Reviewers need to understand risk.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:35.480
Engineers need to confirm technical accuracy.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.820
Compliance teams need to manage status and traceability.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.600
Good evidence management makes artifacts understandable without relying on one person's

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:44.620
memory.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:49.720
It preserves context, source, date, owner, control relationship,

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:53.780
and review status so stakeholders can use the evidence efficiently.
